# PR67 independent Azure networking design review

Review date: 8 November 2026. Base: `9252739eb1bdf7bf6aabc2696ae57000aa02a49f` (verified commit exists). Reviewed input: `/workspace/Azure-learner/docs/LABORATORY_FIREWALL_PART_A.md`, SHA-256 `0388955b901a0d7ad4a770fecccd7f5fbf73a88d5fbdf60997caea3621182591`.

**Disposition: technically viable bounded design; no BLOCKER or MAJOR found in this contract.** Two MINOR specification clarifications appear below. This is a separate preimplementation technical design review, not the required post-stamp Independent Final Audit, implementation acceptance, operational Azure verification, or human SME approval.

I read the applicable permanent, shared-Portal and PR64–66 instructions in `AGENTS.md`, the permanent Quality/Source/Release contracts, the shared Portal contract, the Laboratory platform/server contracts and Networking B/C and Compute A contracts. I inspected the frozen route and NSG code to assess the proposed reuse. I did not edit repository files, implement code, build, run servers, run tests, contact external services, merge or deploy. Only this review artifact was written outside the repository.

## Evidence and provenance

The supplied `/workspace/azurelab-pr67/sources/fetch.json` records 13 Microsoft Learn URLs, HTTP 200 results, final URLs, HTML byte counts, SHA-256 values and page `msDate` metadata. I independently recalculated every saved `*.html.raw` hash and byte count: all 13 match that manifest. The saved text and actual source passages were read, rather than accepting the contract's link titles or `relevant-extracts.txt` alone.

The manifest has no retrieval-time field or HTTP Date/Last-Modified evidence (`lastModified` is null). Its page `msDate` values are publication metadata, not proof of the time fetched. Thus this review verifies the integrity and substance of the supplied source capture; it does not independently attest a fresh network fetch. The contract labels the check date 8 November 2026. This provenance limit is not a discovered technical contradiction.

## Concrete findings

### PR67-DESIGN-01 — MINOR: explicitly label the private-IP allocation algorithm as synthetic

Contract line 12 says, “Private IP is deterministic first usable free address after Azure reserved addresses.” That is a reasonable deterministic simulator rule, but the sentence does not identify it as a simulator allocation policy as clearly as the neighboring policy-scope and priority-uniqueness restrictions do. None of the reviewed Microsoft pages guarantees that Azure Firewall's frontend private IP always equals the first available usable subnet address.

Evidence: [Firewall ARM](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/templates/microsoft.network/azurefirewalls), saved `arm-firewall.txt:895–896`, describes `privateIPAddress` as the private IP associated with Azure Firewall; it does not specify this allocator. [FAQ](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/firewall/firewall-faq), saved `faq.txt:413–419`, explains that the /26 supplies addresses for additional platform VM instances. A single visible private next-hop IP does not model those platform allocations.

Requested clarification: call it a **synthetic deterministic private next-hop address in this simulator**, and state that neither the first-free algorithm nor a single-address platform allocation is asserted for real Azure. Continue to distinguish the persisted Standard/Regional/Static Public IP configuration from an actual publicly allocated address. No algorithm change is requested merely to imitate unmodeled Azure allocation.

### PR67-DESIGN-02 — MINOR: make selected-route and exact endpoint identity checks explicit on every leg

Contract lines 21–27 correctly call for reuse of frozen LPM and source precedence, actual onward/return routes, same-Firewall return configuration and incomplete results for unsupported paths. Tighten line 23 so “return UDR” unambiguously means the **selected** destination-to-source route, not merely any matching stored UDR. Also state that each selected hub onward/return `VnetPeering` route must name the actual destination/source VNet identity.

Evidence: [UDR](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-networks-udr-overview), saved `udr.txt` section “How Azure selects routes,” documents LPM before equal-prefix route-source precedence, and its routing example demonstrates a /24 overriding a /16. Frozen `src/laboratory/v32/analysis.ts:112–127` selects on prefix/source and rejects equal-prefix ambiguous routes. Existing Networking C already protects actual endpoint identity in `src/laboratory/v33/policy.ts` rather than equating any selected peering with arrival.

Concrete counterexamples the implementation must reject: (a) a matching return UDR points at the expected Firewall, but a more-specific selected None/direct/other-Firewall route wins; (b) two individually hub-peered spokes overlap, and the hub's more-specific selected peering points at a VNet other than the selected NIC's VNet. Pairwise hub/spoke nonoverlap validation alone does not prove spoke/spoke disjointness. This is a specification hardening request, not evidence of an implemented bug: implementation has not yet been reviewed.

## Validated Azure semantics and deliberate bounds

| Area | Independent assessment and source evidence |
| --- | --- |
| Policy hierarchy and action | Correct: Policy → Rule Collection Group → Rule Collection → Rule. Collection action applies to all contained rules; rules have no numeric priority/action of their own. `policy.txt:177–201` ([Policy rule sets](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/firewall/policy-rule-sets)). |
| Group/collection ordering | Correct for a network-only policy with no inheritance: ascending group priority, ascending collection priority, terminating match. Both priority ranges are inclusive 100–65000. `processing.txt:177–190` and `295`; `arm-rcg.txt:579–602`. Do not flatten all collections globally by collection priority. Explicit per-parent uniqueness is an acceptable deterministic simulator restriction, not a claimed universal Azure constraint. |
| Rule scope and default deny | TCP/UDP, literal IPv4/CIDR/* and a single destination port/* are narrower than authentic Network Rules. The collection action and no individual rule priority are authentic. Default deny is valid in this network-only, threat-intelligence-Off, no-application/no-inheritance profile; do not generalize it to all real Azure filtering stages. `processing.txt:177–186`, `345–393`; `policy.txt:193–220`; `arm-rcg.txt` NetworkRule schema. |
| Dedicated subnet | Exact `AzureFirewallSubnet` and minimum /26 are supported by `design.txt:284–326` ([Design guide](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/networking/design-guide/azure-firewall)). Ordinary NIC/VM exclusion follows the dedicated service-subnet boundary. The explicit NSG ban matches the service-specific FAQ, `faq.txt:225–227`. Reject invalid existing associations as well as new invalid operations. |
| RG/subscription and Public IP | The specific FAQ explicitly requires Firewall and VNet in the same RG and all Firewall/VNet/Public IP resources in the same subscription; Public IP **may be in another RG**. `faq.txt:391–395`. The contract does not incorrectly impose universal same-RG PIP scope. The Standard/Regional/Static IPv4 combination is represented together in the official Firewall ARM example, `arm-firewall.txt:239–305`; Standard/Static also appears in the deployment example, `firewall-ip.txt:207–222`. One modeled PIP with no allocated public address is a simulator bound, not deployment. |
| Receiving-side forwarding | Correct directions. On a stored local→remote peering, `allowForwardedTraffic` permits the **local VNet to receive** traffic forwarded from the remote VNet. App→Firewall→Data therefore needs Data→Hub true, and Firewall→App established response routing needs App→Hub true. Enabling Hub→Data/Hub→App cannot substitute. `peering.txt:332–335`, with CLI/PowerShell update examples at `452–478` ([Manage peering](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/virtual-network-manage-peering)). Initial App→Hub traffic originated in App; this alone does not require Hub's receiving-forwarded flag. |
| Return route versus new initiation | Correct conditional distinction. The official processing page explicitly says established TCP/UDP responses are permitted even with an explicit reverse-direction Deny, while that Deny still blocks reverse new connections. `processing.txt:359–375` ([Rule processing](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/firewall/rule-processing)). A reverse Allow rule must not be required for the modeled established response. The simulator can inspect configuration needed for a same-Firewall return path; it cannot assert that a connection was established or state actually exists. |
| Private spoke traffic and NAT | Limiting this analysis to RFC1918 endpoints/network rules makes preserving the original source IP consistent with Azure Firewall's default no-SNAT behavior for private destinations. `faq.txt:477–486`; `design.txt:373–375`. Avoid accidentally introducing application-rule SNAT or public-prefix semantics into this bounded path. |
| Onward/return and nontransitivity | Symmetric per-spoke UDR configuration and hub system/peering onward routes are consistent with the official per-spoke routing pattern, `design.txt:284–314`, and FAQ hub/spoke guidance, `faq.txt:466–472`. Forwarding flags do not themselves create routes or transit, explicitly stated at `peering.txt:332–335`. Same-Firewall return checking is a conservative configuration prerequisite, not a sufficient connectivity test. |
| Authentic Azure capabilities excluded | No Firewall-subnet UDRs is a simulator exclusion: authentic Azure may use UDRs there, explicitly described in `faq.txt:496–501`. Same-region/same-subscription Policy association is narrower than real Firewall Policy: the design source describes global cross-region/cross-subscription association at `design.txt:381–389`. Same-region inspected paths are also narrower than globally peered deployments, `faq.txt:182–183`. The contract labels these bounds appropriately. |

## Source disagreements that must remain visible

1. **Firewall naming:** FAQ says 50 characters (`faq.txt:409–411`), while the general resource-name table says 1–80 (`names.txt:1799–1804`). A documented ASCII ≤50 subset is defensible. Do not claim the general Microsoft table says 50 or claim longer names are universally invalid in Azure.
2. **NSGs on “every subnet”:** the general secure hub/spoke source says every subnet (`hub-spoke.txt:438–443`), including surrounding discussion of optional Firewall. That blanket advice conflicts with the explicit Firewall FAQ NSG prohibition. Use the more specific Firewall service rule; the proposed contract correctly does so. This is a real source conflict, not permission to add an NSG to AzureFirewallSubnet.
3. **Default groups:** `policy.txt:178–188` describes three built-in groups with fixed priorities 100/200/300. Custom groups only are a legitimate bounded editing profile, but absence from the modeled inventory must not become a teaching claim that Azure has no default groups. No silent empty groups/default collections are needed to make this simulator path operational. The contract's “no default collection is silently created” is acceptable if presented as simulator behavior.
4. **Default routes and inspection:** `design.txt:291` broadly describes a default UDR as forcing spoke traffic through Firewall, but the same page's concrete table also includes explicit other-spoke prefixes. A 0.0.0.0/0 UDR does not outrank a more-specific direct peering route. The contract's frozen selector and direct-bypass result correctly preserve that distinction; avoid copying the broad prose as an unconditional guarantee.

## Frozen-engine reuse review

The proposed selection-only VirtualAppliance projection is structurally compatible with the frozen selector. In `v32/analysis.ts`, candidate construction, ambiguity detection and winner selection do not use a None next-hop as a special selection preference: prefix length/source/identity determine the winner. Replacing only the unsupported next-hop enum privately, preserving route ID/prefix/source/associated table, then restoring actual Firewall identity and private IP for **all returned candidates and the winner** can therefore reuse selection without inventing persisted None routes.

Required implementation boundary: never feed that projected selected None route into frozen `v33/analysePolicy`, because its higher-level logic classifies None as a drop and rejects appliance transit. Compose the new path from the selected route plus exported `evaluateLayer`/`addressMatch`/`port`, as the design proposes. New wrappers must report model 3.5, never expose projected 3.2/3.3 identity, and fail closed if route restoration cannot identify the real source record.

`v33/policy.ts:101–140` proves only local/direct-peer positive VirtualNetwork tag membership and otherwise returns unknown. `evaluateLayer` at `165–224` uses definite mismatch before unknown, first decisive priority match, and stops on an earlier potentially matching unknown. Reusing these functions avoids a second matcher. A spoke-to-spoke IP can remain unknown under default VirtualNetwork rules because the other spoke is not a direct peer; do not substitute “all RFC1918” or silently widen the tag to make the Firewall scenario green. Explicit literal rules can establish the bounded forward NSG decision. Preserve original source IP across these forward layers.

The historical model dispatch, single PostgreSQL authority, additive SQL006, stable identities/receipts and no course/XP writes are compatible with the permanent Laboratory contracts as specified. This review does not verify their future implementation or migration execution.

## Concrete implementation acceptance cases

These are controls to verify in implementation, not claimed test results:

- Group priority must outrank collection priority across groups; earlier network Deny/Allow terminates; unmatched network-only policy denies. A reverse new-initiation Deny must not be applied as an established-response policy failure.
- Toggle only Data→Hub: the forward path becomes incomplete/allowed according to this flag. Toggle only Hub→Data: it cannot repair the receiving-side failure. Repeat independently for App→Hub on return.
- Verify selected forward and selected return UDRs use the same real Firewall and exact current private next-hop IP. Missing route, another Firewall, more-specific None/direct route, stale IP and same-name different identity must not pass.
- Verify selected hub onward/return peering routes identify the correct endpoint VNets; preserve ambiguous-route failure for equal prefixes and reject a wrong more-specific peer identity.
- Preserve default-route LPM, direct-peering bypass, None drops, unresolved service-tag results, and all existing unsupported-route exceptions. No next-hop placeholder may leak into UI, snapshot, receipt or API success result as a real None route.
- Enforce dedicated-subnet constraints through **existing** NIC/VM/NSG/route-table association commands, including adding a Firewall to a previously occupied or attached subnet. Check all new families against shared capacities and atomic rollback.
- Preserve same command identity/replay, model-bound dispatch, reset/archive/through history, cold/upgrade/repeated SQL paths, concurrent scope isolation, analysis no-write behavior and stale-result invalidation.

No implementation tests, browser/visual verification, final stamp, exact-head CI, anonymous deployed-SHA verification or human review passed as a consequence of this report. The public no-database 503 remains an honest unavailable state, not a functional Firewall Gate7.

