### Actual independent execution and retained outputs

I ran the preserved `/workspace/azurelab-pr67/audit-original-next-hop/reproduce.sh` against the corrected repository modules. Its new stdout is separately retained in `/workspace/azurelab-pr67/next-hop-independent-corrected.json` (SHA-256 `d6b5c601724f11c5a22c25b9766451a4c061dab867a6f43d30287a0f36d82416`). Both original source cases now return `undetermined`, `incomplete`, assessed false and `firewall-next-hop-not-direct`; the equal-prefix lookup remains honestly ambiguous and the more-specific lookup honestly names the wrong hub. The original successful-output artifact was not replaced.

I also wrote and executed `/workspace/azurelab-pr67/next-hop-independent-recheck.cjs` (SHA-256 `b68f9c72617c22874e0cc9bbeaafb9ae599acf4d979ffebcbf7f355a590c1f6f`). All assertions across its 13 focused command-built scenarios passed. Detailed output is `/workspace/azurelab-pr67/next-hop-independent-recheck.json`, SHA-256 `d04c7aaba2e45287fed7e95435ac1049a1869b373eab3ff4998e1dd9568017a1`. Every scenario compares canonical World and entries before/after analysis and confirmed no changes.

| Independent scenario | Corrected observed behavior |
| --- | --- |
| Normal two-spoke baseline | Positive modeled configuration path; both next-hop lookups select direct peering to actual original hub `vnet-2`. |
| Add 0.0.0.0/0 appliance UDR to both spokes, retaining the existing specific workload routes | Positive path retained; both private next-hop lookups select the more-specific direct hub /16. This does not assert that a default route alone overrides all inherited private-space defaults. |
| Source and return, each with a second equal-prefix /16 hub (2 scenarios) | Relevant next-hop route is null with `ambiguous-routes`; specific source/return next-hop-not-direct reason; incomplete and assessed false. |
| Source and return, each with a second more-specific /24 hub (2 scenarios) | Relevant next-hop route names actual wrong hub `vnet-32`; specific source/return next-hop-not-direct reason; incomplete and assessed false. |
| Source and return, each with `/32` None for the Firewall private IP (2 scenarios) | Relevant next-hop output retains actual None; path incomplete and assessed false. No fabricated appliance-placeholder routing drop or positive path. |
| Source and return, each with hub-prefix appliance UDR (2 scenarios) | Relevant output retains actual VirtualAppliance; conservative direct-next-hop prerequisite withholds success. |
| Source NSG Deny; source NSG default-tag Unknown (2 scenarios) | Correct aggregate Deny/Unknown, later destination layers remain not evaluated with accurate `earlier-layer` reason. |
| Firewall explicit Deny | Correct Firewall denial, destination NSG layers remain not evaluated with `earlier-layer`. |

### Code and source assessment

The correction independently resolves `sourceSubnet → actual Firewall.privateIpAddress` and `destinationSubnet → same Firewall.privateIpAddress` through the same restored frozen selector. It requires `VnetPeering` and exact hub `resourceId` on both. Lookup routes/errors are retained as `nextHopRoute`/`nextHopError` and `returnNextHopRoute`/`returnNextHopError`. The source guard executes before forward NSG/Firewall decisions; the return guard executes after selecting the same-Firewall return UDR and before completing the path. No inventory ID bypass remains on a positive branch. Missing/ambiguous/wrong-peer/None/appliance results cannot reach `configurationPathAssessed = true`.

The document's new “Conservative IP next-hop prerequisite” explicitly distinguishes this simulator restriction from a universal recursive Azure UDR rule and preserves the Microsoft broader-prefix NVA counterexample. This correctly addresses the direct-connectivity source requirement without overstating the supplied documentation. The code comment makes the same limitation explicit.

I read the four new permanent all-command regressions: source/return × equal-/more-specific hub. They assert assessed false, the specific failing-leg reason and exact replay. I also inspected the retained red log (four expected failures with the original code) and corrected author log (22/22: 18 pure plus four PostgreSQL). Those are author-run suite records reviewed as evidence; I did not rerun their Vitest/PostgreSQL suites or treat the log as my own execution.

The original copied analysis/topology hashes still match `2520e5c06b9fa1a140ec9d491bfc6b3657e3aa7bc3736f3073433c9698643896` / `7fa39dc3e794dbb3c7e2f116759fa0e33244518aa15cde119276358762d7038c`, preserving the initial defect. No UI server, build, external service, stamp or final audit was run in this correction recheck. Future implementation changes remain subject to their affected checks and the required fresh post-stamp audit.
